Cell[CellGroupData[{Cell[BoxData[InterpretationBox[Cell["\t", "ExampleDelimiter"], $Line = 0; Null]], "ExampleDelimiter"], Cell["On the left of a rule, a selector that looks above its element must be named:", "ExampleText"], Cell[BoxData["page=ImportString[\"<body><nav><a href=\\\"/\\\">Home</a></nav></body>\",{\"HTML\",\"XMLObject\"}];
XMLCases[page,\"nav a\":>1]"], "Input"],

Cell["XMLCases::badpat: The second argument should be a pattern, or a rule pattern -> rhs or pattern :> rhs. A pattern is an XMLPattern, which can take constraints on the elements below it, an XMLDocument pattern, an XMLElement pattern, or a CSS selector string, or alternatives of these, any of them named, with a condition pat /; test or a test pat?f. A CSS selector that looks above or beside the element it selects, such as \"nav a\", gives that element when given alone; on the left of a rule, name the element, as in n : \"nav a\" :> n. Child, Adjacent and Sibling were removed in version 4; nest the patterns as the document is: XMLPattern[\"ul\"][{___, x : XMLPattern[\"li\"], ___}] :> x gives the li children of each ul, XMLPattern[\"nav\"][Descendant[x : XMLPattern[\"a\"]]] :> x the links inside a nav, XMLPattern[_][{___, XMLPattern[\"h2\"], ___, x : XMLPattern[\"p\"], ___}] :> x each p after an h2 among its siblings, and XMLPattern[_][{___, XMLPattern[\"h2\"], x : XMLPattern[\"p\"], ___}] :> x each p right after one. Got \"nav a\".", "Message", "MSG"],

Cell[BoxData["$Failed"], "Output"],

Cell["Name it, and use the name:", "ExampleText"],

Cell[BoxData["XMLCases[page,n:\"nav a\":>HTMLTextContent[n]]"], "Input"],

Cell[BoxData[RowBox[{"{", "\"Home\"", "}"}]], "Output"]}, Open]],

